'''Tèxtils Nacionals Balboa, S.A.''' (TNB) is the largest domestic manufacturer of {{w|textile}}s in [[Balboa]]. The company operates production facilities in [[Sant Cristòfor]] and [[Sant Bart]], manufacturing woven and knitted fabrics, home textiles, technical textiles, and finished garments. In recent decades, TNB has transitioned into a primarily {{w|contract manufacturer|contract manufacturing}} role, producing textiles and garments on behalf of international brands serving the [[BTPMEU]] market. It is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] under the ticker TNB and is a constituent of the [[SCX 100]] index.

The company traces its origins to the colonial-era Companyia Tèxtil Nacional de Volisania, a Volisanian textile manufacturer that operated across Balboa. Following the establishment of [[Zong Balboa]] in 1899, the company was split into two separate entities — Tèxtils Nacionals Balboa in the Zong-controlled territory and Companyia Tèxtil Nacional in the remainder of Balboa. The two companies operated independently for over seven decades before merging following reunification in 1973.

== History ==

=== Colonial origins ===

The Companyia Tèxtil Nacional de Volisania was established in the 1860s as a Volisanian colonial enterprise, operating {{w|cotton mill|cotton mills}} and weaving sheds across Balboa. The company was one of the largest industrial employers in the colony, with production facilities in the canal corridor and the interior provinces. {{w|Cotton}} was imported from Volisanian colonies in [[Tapuya]], and the company supplied textiles to both the domestic market and to Volisanian colonial interests across the region.

=== Split (1899) ===

The [[Treaty of San Lázaro]] of 1899, which transferred [[Sant Cristòfor]] and the canal zone to [[Zong Balboa|Zong control]], divided the company's operations. The facilities within the Zong-controlled territory — including the canal corridor weaving sheds and the Sant Cristòfor finishing operations — were reconstituted as Tèxtils Nacionals Balboa, a Zong Balboan entity. The facilities in the remainder of Balboa — including the interior mills — became Companyia Tèxtil Nacional, a Balboan entity.

The two companies operated independently for the following 74 years. Tèxtils Nacionals Balboa, under Zong administration, lost access to Volisanian colonial cotton supplies from Tapuya and instead imported cotton from [[Sinchew]] and other Zong-associated sources. The company shifted toward synthetic fiber processing as Zong investment modernised the canal corridor's industrial base. Companyia Tèxtil Nacional, in the rump state, continued importing cotton from Tapuya and remained focused on traditional textile production, serving the domestic market of the non-Zong territory.

=== Merger (1973) ===

Following reunification in 1973, the Balboan government initiated the merger of the two successor entities, reuniting the split operations under a single company. The merged company retained the Tèxtils Nacionals Balboa name, as it was the larger of the two operations. The merger created the largest textile manufacturer in Balboa, with production facilities in both the canal corridor and the interior.

The post-merger company inherited a diverse but ageing asset base. During the 1970s and 1980s, the government invested in modernising the combined operations, retiring obsolete equipment and expanding synthetic fiber processing capacity at the canal corridor and Sant Bart facilities. Cotton processing, which had declined in importance relative to synthetic textiles, was gradually phased out.

=== Modernisation and contract manufacturing ===

In the 1990s, TNB benefited from the establishment of the [[BTPMEU]], which opened export markets for Balboan manufactured goods across the economic union. Rather than competing primarily on its own brand, TNB positioned itself as a {{w|contract manufacturer}} for international textile and apparel brands seeking production capacity within the BTPMEU tariff area. The company expanded its production of synthetic fabrics, sourcing fiber from domestic suppliers including [[Opal (Company)|Opal]] and [[Lycan (Company)|Lycan]], and importing cotton primarily from [[Tapuya]].

The company was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1991 as part of the government's privatisation programme. The [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] retained a 12 per cent stake.

In the 2000s, TNB invested in {{w|technical textile}}s, developing industrial fabrics for filtration, automotive, and protective applications. The company also expanded its home textiles division, producing bedding, upholstery, and curtain fabrics for both domestic and export markets.

By the 2020s, contract manufacturing for international brands accounted for the majority of TNB's revenue, with the company producing garments and fabrics under specification for brands selling across the BTPMEU market. Cotton consumption has declined significantly relative to synthetic fibers, reflecting both cost pressures and the shift toward synthetic textile production.

== Operations ==

TNB operates production facilities at two locations:

* [[Sant Cristòfor]] — weaving, knitting, dyeing, finishing, and garment assembly; the largest facility by output
* [[Sant Bart]] — yarn spinning, weaving, and technical textiles

The company sources synthetic fiber from domestic suppliers [[Opal (Company)|Opal]] and [[Lycan (Company)|Lycan]], and imports cotton primarily from [[Tapuya]]. Approximately 55 per cent of output is exported, with the remainder supplying the domestic market.

== Relationship to other textile companies ==

TNB is the largest textile manufacturer in Balboa by revenue and market capitalisation, exceeding competitors such as [[Horaci Eduard Molí]]. While Horaci Eduard Molí operates a single integrated mill in Sant Bart, TNB operates a multi-site network with broader product range and significantly higher output. Both companies source fiber from the same domestic suppliers — [[Opal (Company)|Opal]] and [[Lycan (Company)|Lycan]] — and compete in both domestic and export markets.

